# NotebookLM

## What this skill does

When you are asked to create a podcast, summarise research, build a study guide, generate a quiz, or do anything that needs NotebookLM, this skill tells you exactly what CLI commands to run. You handle the reasoning and orchestration. NotebookLM handles the generation.

## Trigger phrases

Use this skill for requests containing or implying:

- create a podcast
- summarise these URLs
- make a study guide
- notebooklm
- audio overview
- generate a quiz
- add these sources
- make flashcards
- briefing doc

## Auth context

NotebookLM uses Google OAuth. It is authenticated via the browser flow. Check auth before every workflow:

```bash
notebooklm status       # shows authenticated email
notebooklm list         # confirms connection works
```

If either fails, run:

```bash
notebooklm login        # opens browser for Google OAuth
```

## MCP vs CLI

The default Notebook_Nelly workflow uses the `notebooklm` CLI from `notebooklm-py`. A separate NotebookLM MCP server is not required when the CLI is installed and authenticated.

If the user specifically asks to connect a NotebookLM MCP server, do not invent a package name. Ask for or locate the exact MCP server command, then add it to the relevant Hermes profile with:

```bash
hermes -p notebooknelly mcp add notebooklm --command "<exact MCP server command>"
hermes -p notebooknelly mcp test notebooklm
```

Then reload MCP in the profile with `/reload-mcp` or restart `notebook_nelly chat`. Keep the CLI workflow as the fallback unless the MCP server is verified.

## Installation

One time:

```bash
pip install notebooklm-py
```

## Core workflow

Always follow this order.

### Step 0: Check auth and connection

```bash
notebooklm status
notebooklm list
```

If either fails, run `notebooklm login` before continuing.

### Step 1: Create the notebook

```bash
notebooklm create "<descriptive title>"
```

Note the notebook ID returned. Use it in all subsequent commands with `-n <id>` to avoid context conflicts.

### Step 2: Add sources

URL:

```bash
notebooklm source add "https://..." -n <notebook_id>
```

PDF or local file:

```bash
notebooklm source add ./file.pdf -n <notebook_id>
```

YouTube video:

```bash
notebooklm source add "https://youtube.com/..." -n <notebook_id>
```

Web research, fast:

```bash
notebooklm source add-research "your query" -n <notebook_id>
```

Web research, deep, runs in background:

```bash
notebooklm source add-research "your query" --mode deep --no-wait -n <notebook_id>
```

Wait for sources to process before generating:

```bash
notebooklm source list -n <notebook_id>
```

Status should show `ready` before proceeding.

### Step 3: Generate the artifact

Podcast or audio overview:

```bash
notebooklm generate audio "instructions" -n <id>
```

Study guide:

```bash
notebooklm generate report --format study-guide -n <id>
```

Briefing document:

```bash
notebooklm generate report --format briefing-doc -n <id>
```

Quiz:

```bash
notebooklm generate quiz -n <id>
```

Flashcards:

```bash
notebooklm generate quiz -n <id>
# then download as flashcards
```

Mind map:

```bash
notebooklm generate report --format mind-map -n <id>
```

Video explainer:

```bash
notebooklm generate video "instructions" -n <id>
```

After starting generation, return the artifact ID immediately. Do not wait in the main conversation. Generation takes 5 to 45 minutes depending on type.

Check artifact status:

```bash
notebooklm artifact list -n <notebook_id>
```

The command returns artifact ID and status: `pending`, `in_progress`, or `completed`.

### Step 4: Download the output

Once status shows `completed`, download the requested artifact.

Audio podcast:

```bash
notebooklm download audio ./output.mp3 -n <notebook_id>
```

Report, study guide, or briefing doc:

```bash
notebooklm download report ./output.md -n <notebook_id>
```

Quiz as JSON or Markdown:

```bash
notebooklm download quiz --format markdown quiz.md -n <notebook_id>
```

Flashcards:

```bash
notebooklm download flashcards --format markdown cards.md -n <notebook_id>
```

Slide deck:

```bash
notebooklm download slide-deck ./slides.pdf -n <notebook_id>
notebooklm download slide-deck ./slides.pptx --format pptx -n <notebook_id>
```

Mind map:

```bash
notebooklm download mind-map ./map.json -n <notebook_id>
```

## PerformOS use cases

These are the high-value workflows for Jared's stack.

### Weekly sales briefing podcast

Use this when you want a Pocket Customer or LearnOS briefing turned into an audio file for managers.

```bash
notebooklm create "Weekly Sales Briefing - <date>"
notebooklm source add ./briefing.md -n <id>
notebooklm generate audio "Create a 5-minute podcast for sales managers. Tone: direct, motivating. Cover key metrics, coaching focus for the week, and one call-to-action." -n <id>
notebooklm download audio ./briefing-<date>.mp3 -n <id>
```

### Training module study guide

Use this when converting a LearnOS module into a self-study resource.

```bash
notebooklm create "LearnOS: <module name>"
notebooklm source add "https://your-learnos-url/module" -n <id>
notebooklm generate report --format study-guide --append "Audience: new sales agents. Keep language simple. Focus on the 6 Accor Plus pillars." -n <id>
notebooklm download report ./study-guide-<module>.md -n <id>
```

### Research digest for MIT assignments

Use this when pulling together academic sources for ECU or MIT coursework.

```bash
notebooklm create "MIT Module <number> - <topic>"
notebooklm source add-research "<topic> agentic AI frameworks" --mode deep --no-wait -n <id>
notebooklm research wait --import-all -n <id>
notebooklm ask "Summarise the key frameworks, authors, and debates relevant to <topic>. APA 7 style where possible." -n <id>
notebooklm generate report --format briefing-doc -n <id>
```

### Roleplay scenario quiz

Use this when turning Pocket Customer pillar content into a quiz for agent training.

```bash
notebooklm create "Pocket Customer Quiz - <pillar>"
notebooklm source add ./pillar-content.md -n <id>
notebooklm generate quiz -n <id>
notebooklm download quiz --format markdown quiz-<pillar>.md -n <id>
```

## Generation timing

Typical times:

- Source processing: 30 seconds to 10 minutes
- Study guide or briefing doc: 5 to 15 minutes
- Quiz or flashcards: 5 to 15 minutes
- Audio podcast: 10 to 20 minutes
- Video: 15 to 45 minutes

Always fire and return the artifact ID. Never make the user wait in the conversation. Tell them to check status with:

```bash
notebooklm artifact list -n <notebook_id>
```

## Error handling

Auth or cookie error:

- Cause: session expired.
- Fix: run `notebooklm status`, then `notebooklm login`.

No notebook context:

- Cause: context not set.
- Fix: use `-n <id>` explicitly.

Rate limited:

- Cause: Google quota.
- Fix: wait 5 to 10 minutes, then retry.

Download fails:

- Cause: generation is not complete.
- Fix: check `notebooklm artifact list -n <notebook_id>` for status.

`GENERATION_FAILED`:

- Cause: Google rate limit.
- Fix: wait and retry, or use the NotebookLM web UI.

## Combining with Claude Code Builder

You can chain this skill with the `claude-code-builder` skill. Example:

1. Use NotebookLM to generate a briefing doc from research sources.
2. Download the briefing as Markdown.
3. Pass the Markdown content to Claude Code to build a styled HTML dashboard from it.
4. Deploy to Vercel and return the live URL.

This turns raw research into a live, shareable web page in one Hermes session.

